        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Common treatments for orthostatic intolerance, such as fluid loading, fludrocortisone (Florinef), or anti-gravity suits, have only been effective for part of the astronaut population.  One early study showed some improvement in cardiovascular deconditioning by using fluid loading as a countermeasure.  Subsequently, oral fluid and salt loading was adopted as an operational countermeasure for all Shuttle crewmembers.  There have also been upgrades to the launch and entry suits and the addition of liquid cooling garments. Nevertheless, virtually every astronaut returning from space continues to suffer from some degree of orthostatic intolerance, and the incidence of presyncope is still 20% after Shuttle flights. Consequently, there is still a considerable effort to develop countermeasures.  A number of countermeasures are being proposed and/or developed such as pharmacological interventions (Midodrine), exercise regimens, Jobst stockings, or even inflight centrifugation procedures. The entry monitoring procedure will determine the efficacy of various countermeasures with measurements of physiological parameters (heart rate and blood pressure) in real time as gravity levels change from microgravity through hypergravity to earth's gravity during entry, landing and egress.  The effectiveness of proposed inflight countermeasures will be assessed by:<P>  
1. Determining whether orthostatic function is improved during entry, landing, and egress.<br>
2. Investigating the relationship between mission duration, countermeasure efficacy, and changes in orthostatic function of crewmembers during stresses of entry, landing, and egress.<br>
3. Determining whether precautions and countermeasures other than  (or perhaps instead of) the operational saline countermeasure are needed to protect crewmembers in the event of emergency egress.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
As this investigation has been discontinued, the methodology for this experiment is not available at this time.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment has been discontinued; therefore, there are no results available from this experiment. <END>
        </div>

